From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: ECON, GAME THEORY: Re: Justice, Dale Worley, & Libertarian Foundations

   From: eisrael@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)

   > If you really want unregulated, dog-eat-dog competition in the market,
   > why not in the supermarket?

   The two cases are distinct because competition in the market as a whole
   is an instance of non-zero sum interaction, whereas competition for
   places in lines is zero-sum: if you get the first place in line, I
   obviously can't also get the first place in line.

That sounds good for a start, but I think that there's much missing
here.  For instance, any ownership system is zero-sum, in the sense
that whatever I own, you don't.  The non-zero-sum part comes in when
one realizes that the possibility of ownership gives one an incentive
to create ownable things, which is "globally good" in any number of
senses.  (Apply to intellectual property?)

On the other hand, there are non-zero-sum games where open competition
is really bad -- consider the class of "tragedy of the commons
problems".

But all of this seems suspect from an anarcho-capitalist point of view
-- it justifies competition in the name of some sort of global good,
rather than asserting that competition is good by definition.  Do you
really want the legitimacy capitalism to depend on your ability to
*prove* that it gives utilitarian results?

Dale

