Date: Fri, 5 Mar 1993 12:41:53 -0800 (PST)
From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: PHIL/ECON/NANO/NAIVE: Post Scarcity Manifesto

    Well, the reviews of Jay's essay are coming in pretty negative,
I'm sure to nobody's surprise, including his.  Sure enough, there
are some not-very-Extropian sentiments expressed in his post, and
some of 'em presented without much defence or justification.
     But is it maybe a waste of time, and of an interesting post,
to concentrate on the points that there is *obviously* going to
be disagreement about?   Especially if there's an argument more worth 
arguing about in it, which I think there is.
     One view that is held fairly sacred on Extropians is the importance
of *inviolable* private property as a cornerstone of any social/economic
system.  One question that anyone promoting a set of views should be 
able to answer is, *why* do I hold and promote these views?  OK, so
why--in the face of so many people who Know Better, in defiance of
many of our Elected Leaders who are only trying to ensure a Fair and
Just Society, in hopeless opposition to Clear Trends of History (uh,
OK, put that one aside for a while)--WHY are we such assholes on
this silly reactionary point?*
     Well, not everyone will answer this in exactly the same way.
On occasion, one will encounter advocates of this view (sometimes
calling themselves libertarians, sometimes not) for whom it seems
to be--and if this sounds offensive, it is--in effect a religious
tenet, that is, something that doesn't need rational justification.
My impression is that most of the regular participants in Extropian
discussion share my belief that something better than this is necessary.
Here's a pass at my something better (with "my" here not intended to
claim any particular originality for the general line of argument):

Every individual has a biological imperative to attempt to ensure his/her
own survival and that of his/her offspring.  (This isn't, of course, the
only imperative to which we are subject, and is not necessarily always
the one which guides all of our behavior).  This requires access to food.
In all circumstances in which human beings have so far lived, individual
control of necessary implements etc. for obtaining or producing food,
and individual control of food obtained, is the surest way of ensuring
one's own survival and that of one's children.  ("Primitive communism"
isn't; pre-agriculturalists don't worry about land ownership because
land per se has no value in such an economy).  Thus it is impossible
for people to be really secure, satisfied, or happy in a system which
denies them such control.  This remains true today.  The fundamental
political argument against "democratic socialism" is that if the 
government controls the means of production, then in principle it
controls the access of each individual to food and shelter--and where
is "democracy" when your rulers hold your life in their hands?  (Yes,
I know there's an economic argument as well).
     I don't want to put words in Jay's mouth (since he aspires to
make money from his words, I'd need to have my percentage guaranteed),
but I read his essay as an interesting thought experiment.  Imagine
a situation in which there was little or nothing which you needed to
own in order to ensure your access to whatever material support you
might want?  What would be the role of private property in such a
context?  Well, if your devotion to the concept of property is of
the religious sort, then the context shouldn't matter--private property
is intrinsically a Good Thing, even if it doesn't serve any useful
function.  But if your belief in the concept is fundamentally 
utilitarian--which I think it has to be, at least for anyone of a 
materialist turn of mind--then you would expect that in this imaginary
world people wouldn't worry much about it anymore.  And that to me is
the interesting part of Jay's suggestion.  Somebody I think called it 
"pie in the sky" (or something like that, I dumped the post already)--well,
sure.  He started out telling us that he's a science fiction writer.  For
those of you who don't read it, that's what science fiction is like.  That's 
what science fiction is *for*. 


* :-), folks, :-)

