Date: Fri, 5 Mar 93 14:47:03 PST
From: nobody@alumni.cco.caltech.edu
Subject: META: Crypto-Lists

Garrett Goebel asks about anonymity/cryptography on the list.

One thing to ask is, what do you want to keep secret, and from whom?

I don't think it makes sense to try to encrypt list mailings for only 
the "select group" of list subscribers.  What are the limitations on who 
becomes a list member?  There are none, as far as I know.  So we would 
NOT be keeping our messages secret from the White House, the NSA, the 
Democratic Party, or anyone else you would care to mention.  They could 
easily join the list under virtually any mailing address, and they would 
then be given the key to decrypt the messages.

It's a truism that no group greater than 3 people can keep a secret, 
anyway.  Here we'd be asking 150 people to keep a secret.

The other suggest Garrett makes is that this would allow us to keep 
archives of old list messages, such that only people who had list 
memberships back then would be able to read them.  Presumably this would 
mean that the key is changed periodically, and so newcomers to the list 
would not be able to learn the keys used before.

Again, I think this fails on the basis of the difficulty of keeping the 
keys secret.  Both of these suggestions would also have the disadvantage 
of requiring list subscribers to equip themselves with an encryption 
program, and using it for each list message.

Garrett also suggests allowing anonymous postings to the list.  This 
does seem more useful, as it allows individuals to post without fear 
that their identities might be revealed to unfriendly organizations.  
The ANON prefix is worth considering.  The other possibility, which 
might work already, is to use one of the anonymous remailing systems 
recently started up on the net.  These systems strip off sender 
information and pass the message on to a requested destination.  Some 
also support PGP for decryption of forwarded messages.

Send mail to help@anon.penet.fi to learn about Johan Helsingius's 
remailer operating in Finland.  Ftp to soda.berkeley.edu, and look in 
pub/cypherpunks for the file hal's.instructions to find out how to use 
the Cypherpunks remailers.  I'm experimentally posting this message to 
the list via the anonymous remailer running on my own personal account.

