Hmmm.  I can't quite follow all the philosophy.  But it's clear that
one can sell *particular* rights.  Thus one could sign a long-term
indentured-labor contract.  (BTW, due to the Amendment that KMOSTA01
mentions, such contracts are non-enforcable in the U.S.)  (Historically,
it appears that "slavery" in most cultures was more a lifetime
indentured-labor contract than an elimination of the legal existence
of the slave.  For instance, slaves could own property (including
slaves!), and had specific legal rights.)

One can also place one's self under the authority of a set of rules
which are changable over time.  For example, if I join (buy a seat on)
a stock market, my transactions on it are subject to the rules of the
stockmarket, and those rules may change from time to time according to
the legislative processes of the stock market.

It seems that if we allow both of these as LC transactions, one must
admit that one can validly agree to a contract that places one under
almost any sort of authority.  In the extreme case, one could agree to
be bound by the Constitution, laws, executive orders, administrative
rules, judicial interpretations, etc. of the United States.
(Reconstruct the present State by contract!)

Perhaps one could not by contract become an *absolute* slave, but one
could validly come within a hairsbredth of it.

