Date: Fri, 5 Mar 93 15:08:03 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: PHIL/ECON/NANO/NAIVE: Post Scarcity Manifesto

I liked a lot of the points Jay O'Connell made in his post on a "post 
scarcity" world.  Like Jay, I believe that the abundance made possible 
by nanotech manufacturing will cause fundamental changes in how we 
relate to each other economically.

I also see merit in Jay's analogy between nanotech manufacture and 
software (an analogy which Tim May also drew here in one of his first 
messages).  In both cases, information becomes active.  Just as you can 
copy a program and give it to a friend, so you will also be able to copy 
a nanotech design program and give it away.  And I think it's reasonable 
to take the changes in the software market over the last decade as a 
model for how nanotech products will be distributed and paid for.

Of course, nanotech will have even more impact.  Free or "shared" 
software may be useful and helpful, but once that same freedom is 
available for food, clothing, and shelter we can expect fundamental 
changes in our lives.  People will no longer have to work to survive.  
Even the poorest of the poor can have their material needs satisfied.

Years ago, I had a class in which they discussed Maslow's theory that 
human needs could be expessed as a pyramid.  Material needs were at the 
bottom.  As each need was satisfied, higher-level needs became more 
important - things like a feeling of worth, of success, of doing 
something meaningful in life.  I don't recall the details of his 
hierarchy now - maybe someone could post a summary.

Today, much of the world is still floundering at the bottom level of 
Maslow's pyramid.  I expect the advent of nanotech to jack the whole 
world up several levels.  People will be more worried about self-worth 
than filling their bellies.

Now, I recognize, based on earlier discussions here, that there will 
always be some things in short supply.  Some people have a nearly 
insatiable desire for material goods.  And as the world as a whole 
becomes wealthier, the standard by which we judge what is an adequate 
level of material wealth will rise.

What this suggests is that the world of the future may have just as much 
disparity between rich and poor as we have today (or perhaps even more), 
but that the whole range will be shifted far upwards.  The poor of the 
future will be like the rich of today in many ways.

But to assume that this means that this will bring no changes in market 
relationships misses the point, IMO.  Look back at the days of the 
Depression, and compare them with more prosperous times.  In each case 
you have rich and poor.  But the desperation, the fear, the painful 
uncertainty, is absent in times of plenty.  Once people truly have the 
option of having all their material needs met without having to work, 
the world will be a different place.

I don't think Jay is right that markets will completely disappear.  But 
I do think they will become less important than they are today; used 
more by exceptionally motivated or wealthy individuals to seek out 
"intrinsically rare" items, things which for some reason cannot be 
duplicated via nanotech.  Markets are a way of dealing with scarcity.  
As each item become more abundant, it automatically drops out of the 
market system.  This is not in conflict with the extropian principle of 
spontaneous orders; it simply recognizes that there are more forms of 
spontaneous order than markets.  (Our own list is one example.)

An apparent counterexample from fiction:  In Vinge's "A Fire Upon the 
Deep", interstellar civilizations engage in trade.  It was never clear 
to me what was used for money.  The only concrete example I recall was 
the trading of a set of "flamed trellises" for ship repair work.  Flamed 
trellises are lightweight carbon bricks with an internal fractal 
structure, of unknown purpose and origin.  Well, if they had nanotech 
(and there is a little mention of it), why couldn't they make lots of 
duplicates of these flamed trellises?  Why were they scarce?

This seems to me a clear example of an artificial notion of scarcity 
introduced into the story in order to make it more understandable for 
readers of today.  Any attempt to realistically deal with a world of 
nanotech manufacturing (even without such exotica as uploading or brain 
enhancement) will have to take a much harder look at trade: how and when 
would it happen?

I hope Jay will not receive knee-jerk attacks accusing him of calling 
for socialist intervention.  As I read his essay, he is simply 
predicting the nature of voluntary human relationships, basing it on an 
existing model (the software business).  He is not calling for 
governments to step in and make things come out this way.  He is just 
guessing, as we all are.

Hal Finney
